question: Does Fisher's combination of the Shapiro-Wilk and plain S2 p-values (minus two times the sum of log p), calibrated by simulation to exact 5% size at n=400, beat both single tests beyond two paired SE on t df 5 or skew-normal-scaled t df 10 at sqrt(beta1) near 0.3? With 20000 paired draws and a new seed, report its power beside SW, S2, and the either-rejects union, with paired differences and their paired SE.
learning: The either-rejects union of Shapiro-Wilk and plain S2 at exact 5% size and n=400 never beats both single tests beyond two paired SE: across three seeds with 20000 paired draws it lands between SW and S2 wherever one clearly wins (lognormal sigma 0.1, 0.2, skew-normal-scaled t df 10), and on t df 5 it beats SW but only ties S2. Splitting alpha costs the stronger test about what the weaker one adds, so a Bonferroni-style union is not a free gain.
